{#
  Migrations 0120-0128j — THE WELDS.

  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 · §18 (trigger band) · §16 MI01, MI02, MI07, MI09, MI25, MI26, MI27,
  MI29, MI30 · docs/leads/kernel.md D7, D8 · MR-5 (the one filename convention).

  A function is not an invariant. Everything in band 0100-0109 is inert until it is attached to a
  table, and THIS TEMPLATE IS WHERE THE CLAIMS BECOME TRUE. A reviewer auditing whether MI25 holds
  reads 0120, not 0100: the function could be perfect and unwelded and the gate would pass every
  test that does not insert a blocking check.

  ONE STATEMENT PER FILE, WHICH IS WHY THERE ARE SO MANY FILES. CockroachDB DDL is not
  transactional across statements, so a multi-statement file leaves a half-applied version and an
  undiagnosable `dirty` marker. `CREATE TRIGGER` names exactly one table, so an append-only family
  covering eleven tables is eleven statements and therefore eleven files. Under MR-5 the extra ten
  take the lowercase-letter suffix of their own number — the multi-statement slot, which is the
  first of the letter's two legal uses — and they stay inside band 0120-0129z, which the
  allocation grants to this worker exclusively. A worker that runs out of numbers suffixes; it
  never borrows.

  ORDERING WITHIN THE BAND is the order a reviewer should read them in: project before it
  materialises, materialise before it closes, close before it re-opens, verify the chain, guard
  the closure, and only then refuse every mutation. Lexicographic ordering on the whole stem gives
  exactly that, and `0128 < 0128a < 0128b < … < 0129`.

  FIRING ORDER BETWEEN TWO TRIGGERS ON ONE TABLE IS NOT ASSUMED ANYWHERE.
  `blocking_check` carries three row-level triggers after this band: `check_project` BEFORE
  INSERT, `check_materialised` AFTER INSERT, and recall's `bonded_sev5` AFTER INSERT (migration
  0137). PostgreSQL fires row triggers in name order; CockroachDB v26.2 does not document its
  order, and PL-3 forbids resting a dated path on an unproven capability. Nothing here needs it:
  BEFORE always precedes AFTER, which is the only ordering this design depends on, and the two
  AFTER triggers commute — if either raises, the whole statement unwinds in either order.

  THE APPEND-ONLY FAMILY IS THE SUBSTRATE'S SHARE OF §5.11 ITEM 9, AND NOT ALL OF IT.
  §5.11 lists thirty relations. Eleven of them are welded here — the nine SUBSTRATE relations of
  MR-2 that appear on that list, plus the two authority-lineage relations both shipped bindings
  carry. The other nineteen (`ledger_intake`, `ledger_leaf`, `ledger_node`, `ledger_checkpoint`,
  `cosignature`, `blame_edge`, `commit_obj`, `commit_edge`, `silence_ledger`, `silence_receipt`,
  `recall_candidate`, `frontier_move`, `custodian_attestation`, `agent_action`, `drift_finding`,
  `event_severity_revision`, `destruction_record`, `boundary_certificate`,
  `external_attestation`) are VERTICAL objects by MR-1's object test — a second TRAPPOINT vertical
  does not need them to pass `trappoint-conform` — and a template that welded them would emit
  `CREATE TRIGGER` against relations the reference vertical will never own, failing at APPLY time
  in a band nobody would think to look in. They owe an append-only weld in their OWN domain's
  band, against this same function, and each of their migrations already records the debt
  (`0072_ledger_intake.sql` names it explicitly). NAMING THE NINETEEN HERE IS THE POINT: a table
  that owes a weld and has not got one must not be able to hide behind the existence of the
  function.

  `permit`, `change_request` and `disposition` ARE NOT ON THIS LIST, deliberately. The first two
  are the state machine and must be updatable — that is what a merge IS. The third has exactly
  one legal UPDATE and gets its own guard at 0124, whose body is the argument for the exception.

  PLATFORM (measured on CockroachDB CCL v26.2.5, 2026-08-09): `BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE` in one
  `CREATE TRIGGER` is accepted and both arms raise; two triggers on different tables may share a
  name.
#}
